==== NAME
libpdx-audit — audit-first output for paideia-os tools.

==== SYNOPSIS
    module AuditRecord = structure {
      pub let reset       : () -> () !{mem} @{}
      pub let audit_rearm : () -> () !{mem} @{}
    }
    module AuditClient = structure {
      pub let audit_begin        : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap, sched}
      pub let audit_record_output: (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap, sched}
      pub let audit_commit       : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap, sched}
      pub let audit_set_parent   : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{}
      pub let audit_can_emit_output : () -> u64 !{mem} @{}
      pub let audit_last_error   : () -> u64 !{mem} @{}
      pub let audit_broker_failure_cause : () -> u64 !{mem} @{}
    }
    module AuditHash = structure {
      pub let audit_hash_init     : () -> ()  !{mem} @{}
      pub let audit_hash_update   : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{}
      pub let audit_hash_finalize : () -> u64 !{mem} @{}
    }
    module AuditBroker = structure {
      pub let audit_broker_bind   : () -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap}
      pub let audit_send_record   : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap, sched}
    }

==== DESCRIPTION
libpdx-audit is the paideia-os shared library that enforces the D3
audit-first contract: every operation — read as well as write — journals
to `svc.audit-journal` BEFORE it emits any user-visible output. Every
R49/R50 tool links against this library, opens one top-level audit at
_start, records it as work progresses, and commits it before sys_exit.
On any failure of the audit path the tool refuses to emit output and
exits 3 (I4 system-error).

The library ships as three modules — `AuditClient`, `AuditHash`,
`AuditBroker` — over a single `.bss`-resident `AuditRecord` singleton.
The singleton pattern (one audit-in-flight per process at M1..M4) is
deliberate for bootstrap: zero heap dependency, deterministic linkage,
one broker slot cached across every commit in the process. Nested audits
(shell parenting child tools) use `audit_set_parent` at M3-002 to thread
`parent_audit_id` through the wire format; multi-audit-per-process (a
caller-owned `AuditRecord*` variant) lands post-M5 when a consumer needs
it.

See [[cref: WIRE-FORMAT]] for the on-wire event schema, [[cref: STATE-
MACHINE]] for the audit_begin/record_output/commit gating, and [[cref:
RETRY-DISCIPLINE]] for the bounded-retry behaviour on broker EAGAIN.

==== API

---- audit_begin(op_name_ptr, op_args_ptr) -> audit_id
Open a new audit. `op_name_ptr` MUST be non-zero; `op_args_ptr` may be
zero when the tool has no argv beyond op_name. Returns the newly-
allocated `audit_id` (u64 > 0) on success, or 0 on failure (state gate
or send failure — see [[cref: ERROR-CODES]]). On success transitions
`record_state` IDLE → BEGUN and issues an INVOKE event to the broker.

Post-1.0.0 (`#12`): the returned `audit_id` is `(pid << 32) |
local_id`, not a bare per-process counter — see [[cref: WIRE-FORMAT]].
`audit_begin` lazily fetches the process's own pid via `sys_getpid`
(SC+ ID 39) on its first call in a process and caches it; every
subsequent call reuses the cached value at no extra syscall cost.

CAUTION: the failure sentinel is a bare `0`, NOT negative-errno. Every
other IPC-adjacent shim in this org signals failure with a negative
u64 tested via `cmp rax, 0; jl fail`; `audit_begin` does not follow
that idiom, and `cmp rax, 0; jl` against its return is always false —
a consumer that copies the negative-errno pattern here silently treats
every `audit_begin` failure as success. Test `cmp rax, 0; je fail`
instead. Since one sentinel value covers two distinct causes (a state-
gate violation vs. a broker/send failure), call `audit_last_error()`
after a 0 return to disambiguate them.

---- audit_last_error() -> cause
Returns the real cause of the most recent `audit_begin` call:
`AUDIT_OK` (0) if it succeeded or none has run yet, `AUDIT_ERR_STATE`
(1) if the IDLE gate refused it, or the verbatim
`AUDIT_ERR_BROKER_UNAVAILABLE` (3) / `AUDIT_ERR_SEND_FAILED` (4) if its
INVOKE send failed. Only meaningful right after an `audit_begin` call
that returned 0.

---- audit_record_output(audit_id, output_schema_ptr, output_hash) -> err
Record that a schema-typed output stream is about to be emitted. Called
after any use of the streaming hash API ([[cref: HASH]]) — `output_hash`
is typically the return value of `audit_hash_finalize()`. `audit_id`
MUST equal the id returned by the matching `audit_begin`. Optional — a
read-only tool that emits no schema-typed output may skip this and go
straight to commit. On success transitions BEGUN → OUTPUT and issues an
OUTPUT event.

Post-1.0.0 (ENH-003): re-entrant from OUTPUT — a second/third call on
the same open audit is legal (was `AUDIT_ERR_STATE` through v1.0.0)
and emits its own OUTPUT event. A multi-target operation (e.g. `rm a b
c`) may call this once per target instead of losing every target past
the first.

---- audit_commit(audit_id, exit_code) -> err
Close the audit. Legal from BEGUN or OUTPUT; on success transitions to
COMMITTED, issues an EXIT event, then resets to IDLE so a subsequent
`audit_begin` in the same process starts a fresh record.

---- audit_rearm() -> ()
Post-1.0.0 (ENH-004). Clears exactly the per-audit CONTENT slots a
prior audit populated — `record_exit_code`, `record_output_schema_ptr`,
`record_output_hash` — without touching `audit_id_next`,
`audit_broker_slot`, `audit_broker_failed`, or
`record_parent_audit_id`. `audit_begin` calls this automatically right
after its IDLE gate passes, so a second audit in one long-lived process
no longer inherits the previous audit's stale exit code / schema
pointer / output hash into its own INVOKE payload. Also exported
standalone for symmetry with `reset()`.

---- audit_set_parent(parent_audit_id) -> err
Set the parent audit id for the next `audit_begin`. Legal ONLY from
IDLE — parent linkage is a property of the audit's identity and cannot
be mutated mid-flight. Consumers that skip this call get parent = 0
(top-level) via `.bss` zero-init. The shell binds its per-command
`ShellCommandRecord`'s audit_id here before spawning each child tool
(paideia-os plan §5.2, shell.M3-003).

---- audit_can_emit_output() -> safe
Returns 1 only when the sticky broker-failure flag is clear AND an
audit is currently open (`record_state` is BEGUN or OUTPUT); 0
otherwise — including when no audit was ever begun, or one already
committed — and the tool MUST NOT emit any user-visible output; the
caller exits 3. The sticky flag is reset only by `AuditRecord::reset`.

---- audit_broker_failure_cause() -> cause
Post-1.0.0 (ENH-008). Diagnostic-only companion to
`audit_can_emit_output`: once the sticky `audit_broker_failed` flag is
set, this tells `AUDIT_ERR_BROKER_UNAVAILABLE` (bind never resolved)
apart from `AUDIT_ERR_SEND_FAILED` (bind succeeded, send failed).
Returns `AUDIT_OK` before any failure. Sticky-forever policy: only
`AuditRecord::reset` clears it — this is diagnosis, not a recovery
primitive (see [[cref: STICKY-FAILURE-POLICY]]).

---- audit_hash_init / update / finalize
Streaming digest over the tool's output stream. M3-001 ships FNV-1a-64
as a documented placeholder; the primitive swaps to BLAKE3-truncated
when paideia-as v0.33+ exposes BLAKE3 as a stdlib intrinsic ([[cref:
HASH]]). The API surface, the `.bss` state slot, and the return type
are stable across the swap.

==== WIRE-FORMAT
Post-1.0.0, `PdxAuditRecord@0.2` (issues `#11` + `#12`, shipped as one
coordinated wire revision; supersedes `@0.1`). Every lifecycle send
(INVOKE at begin, OUTPUT at record_output, EXIT at commit) marshals a
fixed 256-byte hybrid payload to the kernel `sys_ipc_send` handler:
five u64 header words, then three fixed-size inline NUL-terminated
string fields, then 24 reserved bytes.

    [  0.. 8) audit_id            (record_audit_id — `#12`: (pid << 32) | local_id)
    [  8..16) event_kind          (UEJ_KIND_TOOL_INVOKE=130 |
                                   UEJ_KIND_TOOL_OUTPUT=132 |
                                   UEJ_KIND_TOOL_EXIT=133)
    [ 16..24) exit_code           (record_exit_code; 0 outside EXIT)
    [ 24..32) parent_audit_id     (M3-002; 0 for top-level; opaque
                                   composed id, unaffected by #12's
                                   packing of THIS process's own id)
    [ 32..40) output_hash         (audit_hash_finalize return)
    [ 40..72) op_name             (32-byte inline string; `#11`)
    [ 72..200) op_args            (128-byte inline string; `#11`)
    [200..232) output_schema      (32-byte inline string; `#11`)
    [232..256) reserved           (always zero; never written)

The IPC header word is `0x0000_0100_0000_0220` — op = 0x20
(AUDIT_EVENT), ver = 2 (bumped from 1 at `@0.2`),
reply_endpoint_id = 0 (fire-and-forget), payload_len = 256 (grew from
64 at `@0.2`; was 56 at M2, 64 from M3-002 through v1.0.0).

Through v1.0.0, `op_name_ptr` / `op_args_ptr` / `output_schema_ptr`
were live pointers into caller-owned memory, meaningless outside the
sending process. `#11` fixes this: `audit_send_record` now copies the
string BYTES themselves into the three inline fields above via the
private `AuditBroker::audit_marshal_string` helper — up to
(field_width - 1) source bytes, stopping early at the source's own
NUL, then zero-padded (the field's last byte is always zero, so a
truncated string is still NUL-terminated). A `0` source pointer
(legal for `op_args` before it is set, or `output_schema` before
OUTPUT) yields an all-zero field.

`#12` fixes `audit_id` uniqueness: through v1.0.0 it was a bare
per-process monotonic counter, so every tool's first audit had
`audit_id == 1`, and a `parent_audit_id` of `1` could name ANY
process's first audit. `audit_begin` now composes it as
`(pid << 32) | local_id`, using `sys_getpid` (SC+ ID 39) — already an
existing paideia-os syscall, so `#12` required no new kernel surface,
only a new userspace trampoline in `src/syscall_shim.pdx`.

The byte-for-byte fixture at `tests/goldens/trace_001.md` documents the
canonical `/bin/ls --long /home` audit trace as a shell child (shell
pid 3, local counter 0x2a, so parent_audit_id =
0x000000030000002a; child pid 7, its own first audit, so
audit_id = 0x0000000700000001).

==== STATE-MACHINE
`record_state` transitions:

                       audit_begin
      AUDIT_STATE_IDLE ────────────▶ AUDIT_STATE_BEGUN
                                      │
                                      │ audit_record_output (optional)
                                      ▼
                                    AUDIT_STATE_OUTPUT
                                      │
                                      │ audit_commit
                                      ▼
                                    AUDIT_STATE_COMMITTED
                                      │
                                      │ (reset to IDLE on send return)
                                      ▼
                                    AUDIT_STATE_IDLE

Every entry point validates the state gate BEFORE the audit_id gate;
this ordering surfaces a stale `audit_id` passed after commit as
`AUDIT_ERR_STATE`, not `AUDIT_ERR_ID_MISMATCH`.

Post-1.0.0 (ENH-003): `audit_record_output` is re-entrant from OUTPUT
— OUTPUT is a legal predecessor of itself, each call emitting its own
OUTPUT event. Purely additive; no wire-format change, no new error
code.

==== ERROR-CODES
    AUDIT_OK                    = 0
    AUDIT_ERR_STATE             = 1   State-machine gate refused.
    AUDIT_ERR_ID_MISMATCH       = 2   audit_id != record_audit_id.
    AUDIT_ERR_BROKER_UNAVAILABLE= 3   svc_lookup for svc.audit-journal
                                      returned EINVAL/EFAULT/ENOENT/ENOSPC.
    AUDIT_ERR_SEND_FAILED       = 4   sys_ipc_send retries exhausted or a
                                      non-EAGAIN failure. Sets sticky
                                      `audit_broker_failed`; caller MUST
                                      check `audit_can_emit_output` before
                                      any output.
    AUDIT_ERR_HASH_INACTIVE     = 5   audit_hash_update called without a
                                      prior audit_hash_init.

==== RETRY-DISCIPLINE
`audit_send_record` wraps `sys_ipc_send` in a bounded retry loop: 3
attempts on `SYS_IPC_SEND_ERR_EAGAIN (1)`, with a fixed 4096-cycle spin
backoff between attempts. Every other non-zero return (BAD_ID /
PAYLOAD_LEN / EFAULT / CHANDEAD) hard-fails immediately. Exhausted
retries set the sticky `audit_broker_failed` flag and return
`AUDIT_ERR_SEND_FAILED`; the caller MUST exit 3 without emitting any
user-visible output.

==== STICKY-FAILURE-POLICY
Post-1.0.0 (ENH-008). `audit_broker_failed` is set by either failure
epilogue of `audit_send_record` and cleared ONLY by `AuditRecord::
reset` — never by `audit_rearm` (see [[cref: STATE-MACHINE]]) and
never by any other entry point. This is an explicit policy decision,
not an oversight: under D3 audit-first, a broker/journal failure is
not a condition a process should recover from and keep emitting
output under — a supervisor that restores the broker restarts the
affected tool. A per-audit clear of "I failed to journal" would let a
tool route around one failed send by opening a fresh audit, defeating
D3.

`audit_broker_failure_cause()` is a companion diagnostic-only
accessor (not a recovery primitive) distinguishing which of the two
`audit_send_record` failure epilogues set the sticky flag:
`AUDIT_ERR_BROKER_UNAVAILABLE` (bind never resolved a slot) versus
`AUDIT_ERR_SEND_FAILED` (bind succeeded, the send's retry budget
exhausted or hard-failed). Both slots are zeroed together, only by
`reset`.

==== PARENT-LINKAGE
The parent-child linkage between a shell's `ShellCommandRecord` and its
spawned child tools travels through the `parent_audit_id` slot at
payload offset 24 (offset 56 through v1.0.0, before the `@0.2` grow —
see [[cref: WIRE-FORMAT]]). The shell binds its command-record's audit_id via
`audit_set_parent(shell_cmd_id)` in the child's process image before
_start, and the child's every INVOKE/OUTPUT/EXIT send carries that id.
A top-level audit (a login shell, or a tool spawned outside a shell)
has parent = 0.

`audit_set_parent` refuses non-IDLE state with `AUDIT_ERR_STATE` —
identity mutations mid-flight would break replay assumptions.
[[cref: shell#ShellCommandRecord]] documents the shell-side binding.

==== HASH
`AuditHash` exposes a streaming digest API — init/update/finalize. At
M3-001 the primitive is FNV-1a-64 seeded with FNV_OFFSET_BASIS
(0xcbf29ce484222325) and stepped by FNV_PRIME (0x100000001b3). The
primitive swaps to BLAKE3-truncated (BLAKE3-256, upper 64 bits) when
paideia-as v0.33+ exposes BLAKE3 as a stdlib intrinsic. The API
surface, the `.bss` slot, and the return type are stable across the
swap; only the empty-stream self-check constant changes (BLAKE3 has
its own IV/state-init contract).

==== POSIX-DIFFERENCES
[[posix: no analog]] POSIX tools have no notion of a supervisor-visible
audit journal that must land before any user-visible output. The
closest ancestor is Linux `auditd` — but auditd is (a) kernel-driven
via LSM hooks, not opt-in per tool, and (b) advisory: a tool that
suppresses stdout without emitting an audit event is not detectable
by an auditd consumer. libpdx-audit inverts both properties: userspace
tools opt in by linking against the library, and the library refuses
to let the tool emit output when the journal path is broken.

[[posix: no analog]] The three-call API (`begin` / `record_output` /
`commit`) has no POSIX equivalent. The closest shape is the SUS-3 POSIX
audit primitives from `aud_*` (deprecated in 2004), which lacked
parent linkage and had no retry discipline.

==== EXAMPLES
Consumer wire-up from `design/architecture.md` §1:

    AuditRecord::reset()                       # bootstrap init
    let id  = AuditClient::audit_begin(op_name, args)
    AuditHash::audit_hash_init()
    ... tool computes work and folds output bytes into the hash ...
    let hh  = AuditHash::audit_hash_finalize()
    let err = AuditClient::audit_record_output(id, schema, hh)
    if err != 0 { sys_exit(3) }
    ... tool emits output ...
    let err = AuditClient::audit_commit(id, exit_code)
    if err != 0 { sys_exit(3) }

Shell parenting a child (paideia-os plan §5.2, shell.M3-003):

    # in the shell's per-command block, before sys_execve:
    AuditClient::audit_set_parent(shell_cmd_id)
    sys_execve(child, argv, envp)

